#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# weierstrass_zeta

#% text
# `weierstrass_zeta(u, [g2, g3])` gives the Weierstrass zeta function `zeta(u; g2, g3)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `zeta' = -p` and `zeta(u) - 1/u` vanishes at 0 (DLMF 23.2.5); `zeta` is odd and quasi-periodic, `zeta(u + 2 omega) = zeta(u) + 2 eta(omega)`.

#% item
# Numerical values come from the theta series of `theta_1'/theta_1` (DLMF 23.6.9) at the reduced argument, carried back by the quasi-periods.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
weierstrass_zeta(0.5, [1, 2])
#% output
#= 1.997469078837566

#% cell
n(weierstrass_zeta(1/3, [1, 2]), 30)
#% output
#= 2.99932386696384015173368615688117

#% cell
weierstrass_zeta(7.3 - 4.1*im, [1, 2])
#% output
#= 3.436884082023576 + 1.4539606319836558im

#% cell
weierstrass_zeta(x, [0, 0])
#% output
#= x^-1

#% text
# `zeta' = -p` by a difference quotient:

#% cell
(weierstrass_zeta(0.8 + 1.0e-6, [1, 2]) - weierstrass_zeta(0.8 - 1.0e-6, [1, 2]))/2.0e-6 + weierstrass_p(0.8, [1, 2])
#% output
#= 3.697686601356054e-11

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `weierstrass_sigma`, `weierstrass_p`
